-- GitLab -->> database backed web application


GitLab is a web-based DevOps platform that helps developers manage software projects. It combines Git-based version control with tools for collaboration, automation, and deployment.

Here's what GitLab is commonly used for:

Source code management: Store and track code using Git.
Collaboration: Multiple developers can work on the same project, create branches, merge changes, and review each other's code.
Issue tracking: Manage bugs, feature requests, and project tasks.
CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment): Automatically build, test, and deploy applications whenever code changes are pushed.
Code reviews: Use merge requests (similar to GitHub pull requests) to review and discuss code before merging.
Security and monitoring: Scan code for vulnerabilities and monitor application performance.

