5. git apply
git apply takes a patch and changes your working files.
For example:
git apply patch.patch
It modifies your files, but does not create a commit automatically.
So afterward:
git add .
git commit -m "Add login feature"
The process is:
patch
 ↓
git apply
 ↓
files changed
 ↓
git add
 ↓
git commit
6. Checking a Patch Before Applying It
You can first ask Git:
"Will this patch apply cleanly?"
Use:
git apply --check patch.patch

If nothing is printed:

(no output)
that's generally good.
If you see:
error: patch failed

the patch doesn't apply cleanly.
This is useful because you can check first without changing your files.

git am 

it apply the commit for the contributor unlike git apply in which we have to make the commit manually

8. What If git am Has a Conflict?

Suppose the contributor's patch expects:
hello
world
But your project now has:
hello
Git
world
Git may not know where to apply the patch.
You'll get a conflict.
Git tells you something like:
Patch failed
Now you manually fix the conflict.
Then:

git add .
git am --resolved
Git continues applying the patch.
If You Don't Want the Patch
You can skip it:
git am --skip
Meaning:
"Don't apply this patch. Move to the next one."
If You Want to Cancel Everything
Use:

git am --abort
Meaning:
"Forget this patch operation and return me to the previous state."
So remember:
Conflict during git am
        │
        ├── Fix conflict → git add → git am --resolved
        │
        ├── Don't want patch → git am --skip
        │
        └── Cancel everything → git am --abort

