git add -i

You can enter Git's interactive staging mode with:

git add -i

Git gives you a menu like:

*** Commands ***
1: status
2: update
3: revert
4: add untracked
5: patch
6: diff
7: quit
8: help

You can think of these as:

Command	Meaning
1 / s	See status
2 / u	Stage files
3 / r	Unstage files
4 / a	Add untracked files
5 / p	Stage only parts of files
6 / d	See staged changes
7 / q	Quit
4. Stage specific files

Suppose you have:

TODO
index.html
simplegit.rb

and all three have changes.

You only want:

TODO
index.html

to be staged.

Run:

git add -i

Then choose:

2

because 2 means update.

Git shows:

1: TODO
2: index.html
3: simplegit.rb

You enter:

1,2

Then press Enter.

Now:

TODO          → staged
index.html    → staged
simplegit.rb  → unstaged

So your next commit will contain only:

TODO
index.html
5. What does "unstage" mean?

Suppose you accidentally staged:

TODO
index.html

but you don't want TODO anymore.

You can unstage it.

Choose:

3

because 3 = revert.

Then:

1

to select TODO.

After pressing Enter:

TODO          → unstaged
index.html    → staged

Important:

Unstaging does NOT delete your changes.

It simply moves the changes out of the staging area.

6. See what you staged

You can choose:

6

which means diff.

This shows you exactly what will be included in your next commit.

For example:

- <div id="footer">contact : support@github.com</div>
+ <div id="footer">contact : email.support@github.com</div>

This is useful because before committing you can ask:

"Exactly what am I about to commit?"

7. The REALLY useful part: Partial staging

This is the most important concept in this section.

Imagine you edit one file:

student.cpp

But you make two completely different changes.

For example:

// Change 1
int age = 20;

and later:

// Change 2
cout << "Hello";

You want:

Commit 1 → age change
Commit 2 → Hello change

But both changes are inside the same file.

Normally:

git add student.cpp

would stage both changes.

But you can stage only one.

8. git add -p

This is probably the command you should remember most:

git add -p

p means:

patch

Git shows you a section of your changes called a hunk.

For example:

- command("git log -n 25 #{treeish}")
+ command("git log -n 30 #{treeish}")

Git asks:
Stage this hunk [y,n,a,d,s,e,?]?
Now you choose what to do.
9. The important options

You don't need to memorize every option initially.
Focus on these:
y → Yes
y
Means:
Stage this change.
n → No
n
Means:
Don't stage this change.
The change stays in your working directory.
a → All
a
Means:
Stage this hunk and all remaining hunks in this file.

d → Don't stage
d

Means:
Don't stage this hunk or the remaining hunks in this file.
s → Split
s

Sometimes Git combines several small changes into one big hunk.

s tries to split it into smaller hunks.
This is extremely useful when you want very precise commits.
e → Edit manually
e
Allows you to manually decide which lines of the hunk should be staged.

This is more advanced, so don't worry about it yet.
10. Simple example
Suppose you modify main.cpp:
+ int age = 20;
...
+ cout << "Hello";
You want the first change in Commit 1 and the second change in Commit 2.
Run:
git add -p
Git shows the first hunk:
Stage this hunk? [y,n,...]
You type:
y
Then Git shows the second:
Stage this hunk? [y,n,...]
You type:
n
Now:
Staged:
    int age = 20;
Unstaged:
    cout << "Hello";
Then:
git commit -m "Add age variable"

Only the first change is committed.

Later:

git add .
git commit -m "Print hello message"

The second change gets its own commit.

11. Why is this useful?

Imagine you're working on a project and accidentally make these changes:

login bug fix
+
new button
+
README update
+
debugging code
If you simply do:
git add .
git commit -m "changes"
you get one messy commit.

Instead:
Commit 1
"Fix login bug"
Commit 2
"Add new button"
Commit 3
"Update README"
This makes your Git history clean and understandable.
This is especially useful when contributing to open-source projects because maintainers prefer focused commits.
12. git add -i vs git add -p
The easiest way to remember:
Interactive mode
git add -i

Gives you a menu:

status
update
revert
patch
diff
...
You interact with Git through that menu.
Patch mode
git add -p
Directly takes you to partial staging.
So if your goal is simply:
"I changed multiple things in one file, but I only want to commit some of them."
Use:
git add -p
